('b1000001-0000-0000-0000-000000000005',
'Concept Overview: Trigonometry',
E'## What Is Trigonometry?\n\nTrigonometry is the branch of mathematics that studies the relationships between the sides and angles of triangles. The word comes from the Greek: "trigonon" (triangle) + "metron" (measure).\n\nIn WASSCE Mathematics, trigonometry typically carries 8-12 marks across papers. It is used in problems involving:\n- Finding unknown sides and angles in right-angled triangles\n- Solving triangles using the Sine Rule and Cosine Rule\n- Working with trigonometric identities and equations\n- Applications to bearings and heights/distances\n\n### Why Trigonometry Is Fundamental\n\nTrigonometry connects geometry and algebra. Once you understand how angles relate to ratios of sides, you can:\n- Find the height of a mountain without climbing it\n- Calculate the distance across a river\n- Design bridges, buildings, and roads\n- Understand wave motion in physics\n- Solve navigation problems\n\n### The Six Trigonometric Ratios\n\nFor a right-angled triangle with an acute angle θ:\n\n**Primary ratios**: sin θ, cos θ, tan θ\n**Reciprocal ratios**: cosec θ (= 1/sin θ), sec θ (= 1/cos θ), cot θ (= 1/tan θ)\n\n### WASSCE Focus Areas\n\n1. SOH-CAH-TOA for right triangles\n2. Trigonometric ratios of special angles (30°, 45°, 60°, 90°)\n3. Sine Rule and Cosine Rule\n4. Area formula: Area = (1/2)ab sin C\n5. Trigonometric identities\n6. Graphs of sin, cos, tan\n7. Solving trigonometric equations\n\n### Angle Measurement\n\nAngles can be measured in **degrees** (°) or **radians** (rad).\n- Full circle = 360° = 2π radians\n- 180° = π radians\n- 90° = π/2 radians\n- Conversion: degrees = radians × (180/π)\n- Conversion: radians = degrees × (π/180)',
'overview', 1),

('b1000001-0000-0000-0000-000000000005',
'Definitions & Key Terminologies',
E'## All Essential Trigonometry Terms\n\n### Triangle Components\n\n**Hypotenuse**: The longest side in a right-angled triangle; the side opposite the right angle.\n**Opposite**: The side directly opposite the angle of interest θ.\n**Adjacent**: The side next to the angle of interest that is NOT the hypotenuse.\n\n### The Primary Ratios (SOH-CAH-TOA)\n\n**Sine**: sin θ = Opposite / Hypotenuse (SOH)\n**Cosine**: cos θ = Adjacent / Hypotenuse (CAH)\n**Tangent**: tan θ = Opposite / Adjacent (TOA)\n\nMemory aid: **SOH-CAH-TOA**\n\n### The Reciprocal Ratios\n\n**Cosecant**: cosec θ = 1/sin θ = Hypotenuse/Opposite\n**Secant**: sec θ = 1/cos θ = Hypotenuse/Adjacent\n**Cotangent**: cot θ = 1/tan θ = Adjacent/Opposite\n\n### Special Angles Table\n\n| Angle | 0° | 30° | 45° | 60° | 90° |\n| sin | 0 | 1/2 | √2/2 | √3/2 | 1 |\n| cos | 1 | √3/2 | √2/2 | 1/2 | 0 |\n| tan | 0 | 1/√3 | 1 | √3 | undefined |\n\n### Signs in the Four Quadrants (CAST Rule)\n\nThe CAST rule tells you which ratios are positive in each quadrant:\n- **Quadrant 1** (0° to 90°): **All** positive (A)\n- **Quadrant 2** (90° to 180°): **Sine** positive (S)\n- **Quadrant 3** (180° to 270°): **Tangent** positive (T)\n- **Quadrant 4** (270° to 360°): **Cosine** positive (C)\n\nMemory: **C-A-S-T** going anticlockwise from Quadrant 4, or "**All Students Take Calculus**" going clockwise from Quadrant 1.\n\n### Key Identities\n\n**Pythagorean Identity**: sin²θ + cos²θ = 1\n(Derived from Pythagoras: O² + A² = H²; divide both sides by H²)\n\n**Derived identities**:\n- 1 + tan²θ = sec²θ\n- 1 + cot²θ = cosec²θ\n\n**Ratio Identity**: tan θ = sin θ / cos θ\n\n### The Sine Rule\n\na/sin A = b/sin B = c/sin C\n\nWhere a, b, c are sides opposite to angles A, B, C respectively.\n\n**Use when you know**: Two angles and one side, OR two sides and a non-included angle.\n\n### The Cosine Rule\n\na² = b² + c² - 2bc cos A\n\n**Rearranged to find an angle**:\ncos A = (b² + c² - a²) / (2bc)\n\n**Use when you know**: Three sides, OR two sides and the included angle.\n\n### Area Formula\n\nArea of triangle = (1/2) × a × b × sin C\n\nWhere a and b are two sides and C is the included angle between them.',
'definitions', 2),

('b1000001-0000-0000-0000-000000000005',
'Deep Explanation: Trigonometry Methods',
E'## Complete Method Guides\n\n### Part 1: Solving Right-Angled Triangles\n\nA right-angled triangle has one 90° angle. To solve it, you need at least two pieces of information (including one side).\n\n**Strategy — Finding a Side**:\n1. Identify the angle you are using (not 90°)\n2. Label the sides: hypotenuse (H), opposite (O), adjacent (A)\n3. Choose the correct ratio (SOH-CAH-TOA)\n4. Set up the equation and solve\n\n**Strategy — Finding an Angle**:\n1. Identify which two sides you know\n2. Set up the ratio\n3. Use the inverse function: sin^(-1), cos^(-1), or tan^(-1)\n\n#### Finding side x: Angle = 35°, adjacent side = 8 cm\nWe need: opposite (x), we have: adjacent (8), angle = 35°\nRatio: tan(35°) = x/8\nx = 8 × tan(35°) = 8 × 0.7002 = 5.60 cm\n\n#### Finding angle θ: opposite = 5, hypotenuse = 13\nsin θ = 5/13 = 0.3846\nθ = sin^(-1)(0.3846) = 22.6°\n\n### Part 2: Trigonometric Ratios of Obtuse Angles\n\nFor angles between 90° and 180° (obtuse angles), use the CAST rule:\n- sin(180° - θ) = sin θ\n- cos(180° - θ) = -cos θ\n- tan(180° - θ) = -tan θ\n\n**Example**: Find sin(150°) and cos(150°)\n150° = 180° - 30°\nsin(150°) = sin(30°) = 1/2\ncos(150°) = -cos(30°) = -√3/2\n\n### Part 3: The Sine Rule — When and How\n\nUse the Sine Rule when you know:\n- Case 1: Two angles and any side (AAS or ASA)\n- Case 2: Two sides and a non-included angle (SSA — watch for ambiguous case)\n\n**Applying Sine Rule to find a side**:\na/sin A = b/sin B\nCross-multiply: a × sin B = b × sin A\nSolve for the unknown\n\n**Applying Sine Rule to find an angle**:\nsin A/a = sin B/b\nsin A = a × sin B / b\nA = sin^(-1)(a × sin B / b)\n\n**Ambiguous Case (SSA)**: When you use the Sine Rule with two sides and a non-included angle, there may be TWO possible triangles. Check by seeing if both θ and 180° - θ give valid triangles (all angles must be positive and sum to 180°).\n\n### Part 4: The Cosine Rule — When and How\n\nUse the Cosine Rule when you know:\n- Case 1: Three sides (SSS) — to find an angle\n- Case 2: Two sides and the included angle (SAS) — to find the third side\n\n**Finding a side (SAS)**:\na² = b² + c² - 2bc cos A\n→ Calculate the right side, then take the square root\n\n**Finding an angle (SSS)**:\ncos A = (b² + c² - a²) / (2bc)\n→ Calculate the fraction, then apply cos^(-1)\n\n**Important**: After using Cosine Rule to find one angle, use Sine Rule for remaining angles (simpler arithmetic).\n\n### Part 5: Trigonometric Identities\n\nIdentities are equations true for ALL values of the variable. They are used to simplify expressions and prove other results.\n\n**Core Identity**: sin²θ + cos²θ = 1\n\nRearrangements:\n- sin²θ = 1 - cos²θ\n- cos²θ = 1 - sin²θ\n\n**Strategy for Proving Identities**:\n1. Work on ONE side only (usually the more complex side)\n2. Use substitutions from the core identity and ratio identity\n3. Simplify until it matches the other side\n4. NEVER move terms across the equals sign\n\n#### Prove: tan θ × cos θ = sin θ\nLHS = tan θ × cos θ\n= (sin θ/cos θ) × cos θ\n= sin θ\n= RHS ✓\n\n### Part 6: Solving Trigonometric Equations\n\nA trigonometric equation has infinitely many solutions, so you are usually given a range (e.g., 0° ≤ θ ≤ 360°).\n\n**Four-step method**:\n1. Isolate the trig ratio\n2. Find the principal value (the calculator value)\n3. Use CAST to find all solutions in the range\n4. List all solutions\n\n**Example**: Solve sin θ = 0.5 for 0° ≤ θ ≤ 360°\nStep 1: sin θ = 0.5 (already isolated)\nStep 2: Principal value = sin^(-1)(0.5) = 30°\nStep 3: Sine is positive in Quadrants 1 and 2\nQ1: θ = 30°\nQ2: θ = 180° - 30° = 150°\nSolutions: θ = 30° or θ = 150°\n\n**Example**: Solve cos θ = -0.6 for 0° ≤ θ ≤ 360°\nStep 1: cos^(-1)(0.6) = 53.1° (ignore negative for now)\nStep 2: Cosine is negative in Quadrants 2 and 3\nQ2: θ = 180° - 53.1° = 126.9°\nQ3: θ = 180° + 53.1° = 233.1°\nSolutions: θ = 126.9° or θ = 233.1°',
'explanation', 3),

('b1000001-0000-0000-0000-000000000005',
'Worked Examples: Trigonometry',
E'## Eight Worked Examples (Basic to Exam-Standard)\n\n### Example 1 — Basic: Find the value of sin 60° × cos 30° - cos 60° × sin 30°\n\nUsing the special angle table:\nsin 60° = √3/2, cos 30° = √3/2, cos 60° = 1/2, sin 30° = 1/2\n\n= (√3/2)(√3/2) - (1/2)(1/2)\n= 3/4 - 1/4\n= 2/4\n= 1/2\n\n**Answer: 1/2** (Note: this equals sin 30°, illustrating the compound angle formula sin(A-B))\n\n---\n\n### Example 2 — Basic: Find angle θ in a right triangle where the opposite = 7 and hypotenuse = 25\n\nsin θ = opposite/hypotenuse = 7/25 = 0.28\nθ = sin^(-1)(0.28) = 16.26°\n\n**Answer: θ ≈ 16.3°**\n\n---\n\n### Example 3 — Intermediate: In triangle ABC, find side BC given AB = 12 cm, angle A = 40°, angle B = 75°\n\nStep 1: Find angle C: C = 180° - 40° - 75° = 65°\nStep 2: Using Sine Rule (side BC is opposite angle A = 40°; side AB is opposite angle C = 65°)\nBC/sin A = AB/sin C\nBC/sin 40° = 12/sin 65°\nBC = 12 × sin 40°/sin 65°\nBC = 12 × 0.6428/0.9063\nBC = 7728/9063\nBC ≈ 8.52 cm\n\n**Answer: BC ≈ 8.52 cm**\n\n---\n\n### Example 4 — Intermediate: Find the area of triangle PQR given PQ = 10 m, PR = 7 m, angle P = 55°\n\nUsing Area = (1/2) × a × b × sin C\nArea = (1/2) × PQ × PR × sin P\n= (1/2) × 10 × 7 × sin 55°\n= (1/2) × 10 × 7 × 0.8192\n= 28.67 m²\n\n**Answer: Area ≈ 28.7 m²**\n\n---\n\n### Example 5 — Intermediate: Prove the identity: (sin θ + cos θ)² = 1 + 2sin θ cos θ\n\nExpand the left side:\nLHS = sin²θ + 2sin θ cos θ + cos²θ\n= (sin²θ + cos²θ) + 2sin θ cos θ\n= 1 + 2sin θ cos θ\n= RHS ✓\n\n**Proven.**\n\n---\n\n### Example 6 — Challenging: Solve 2sin²θ - sin θ - 1 = 0 for 0° ≤ θ ≤ 360°\n\nStep 1: Treat as quadratic in sin θ\nLet u = sin θ:\n2u² - u - 1 = 0\nStep 2: Factorise\n(2u + 1)(u - 1) = 0\nu = -1/2 or u = 1\nStep 3: Solve for θ\n\nCase 1: sin θ = 1\nθ = 90°\n\nCase 2: sin θ = -1/2\nReference angle = sin^(-1)(1/2) = 30°\nSine is negative in Q3 and Q4:\nθ = 180° + 30° = 210° or θ = 360° - 30° = 330°\n\n**Answers: θ = 90°, 210°, 330°**\n\n---\n\n### Example 7 — Challenging: In triangle ABC, a = 8, b = 11, c = 14. Find the largest angle.\n\nThe largest angle is opposite the longest side, so we find angle C (opposite c = 14).\n\ncos C = (a² + b² - c²)/(2ab)\n= (64 + 121 - 196)/(2 × 8 × 11)\n= (185 - 196)/176\n= -11/176\n= -0.0625\n\nC = cos^(-1)(-0.0625) = 93.6°\n\n**Answer: The largest angle C ≈ 93.6°**\n\n---\n\n### Example 8 — Exam Standard: A ship sails from port P, 30 km due North to point Q. It then changes course and sails 40 km in the direction N 60° E to point R. Find the distance PR and the bearing of R from P.\n\nStep 1: Sketch the triangle PQR\nPQ = 30 km (North)\nQR = 40 km at N 60° E\nAngle PQR = 180° - 60° = 120° (interior angle)\n\nStep 2: Find PR using Cosine Rule\nPR² = PQ² + QR² - 2(PQ)(QR)cos(angle PQR)\n= 900 + 1600 - 2(30)(40)cos(120°)\n= 2500 - 2400(-0.5)\n= 2500 + 1200\n= 3700\nPR = √3700 ≈ 60.8 km\n\nStep 3: Find angle QPR using Sine Rule\nsin(QPR)/QR = sin(PQR)/PR\nsin(QPR) = 40 × sin(120°)/60.8\n= 40 × 0.8660/60.8\n= 0.5697\nAngle QPR = sin^(-1)(0.5697) = 34.8°\n\nStep 4: Find bearing of R from P\nBearing starts from North, measured clockwise\nBearing = 034.8° ≈ 035°\n\n**Answers: PR ≈ 60.8 km; Bearing of R from P ≈ 035°**',
'examples', 4),

('b1000001-0000-0000-0000-000000000005',
'Common Mistakes, Practice Questions & Solutions',
E'## Common Mistakes to Avoid\n\n1. **Using SOH-CAH-TOA on non-right triangles**: SOH-CAH-TOA ONLY works in right-angled triangles. Use the Sine Rule or Cosine Rule for other triangles.\n\n2. **Forgetting the second solution**: For sin θ = 0.5, students often write only θ = 30°. Remember to check ALL quadrants for the full range given.\n\n3. **Using the Sine Rule when the Cosine Rule is needed**: If you know all three sides or two sides with the INCLUDED angle, you must use the Cosine Rule.\n\n4. **Ambiguous case oversight**: In the SSA case with Sine Rule, always check if 180° minus your answer also forms a valid triangle.\n\n5. **Special angle errors**: Students often mix up sin 30° = 1/2 and sin 60° = √3/2. Learn the table by heart.\n\n6. **Ignoring units**: Angles must be in degrees when using your calculator in degree mode, or radians in radian mode. Check your calculator setting.\n\n7. **Wrong identity manipulation**: sin²θ ≠ (sin θ)². They are the same thing — but students sometimes miswrite it. Also sin(2θ) ≠ 2sin θ unless θ = 0°.\n\n---\n\n## Practice Questions\n\n### Section A: Multiple Choice\n\n**Question 1**: What is the value of tan 45°?\n(A) 0  (B) 1/2  (C) 1  (D) √2\n\n**Question 2**: In a right triangle, if the opposite = 5 and hypotenuse = 13, what is cos θ?\n(A) 5/13  (B) 12/13  (C) 5/12  (D) 13/12\n\n**Question 3**: In which quadrant is cos θ negative and sin θ positive?\n(A) Quadrant 1  (B) Quadrant 2  (C) Quadrant 3  (D) Quadrant 4\n\n**Question 4**: Using Cosine Rule, if a = 5, b = 7, C = 60°, find c.\n(A) √29  (B) √39  (C) 6  (D) √74\n\n**Question 5**: Solve sin θ = √3/2 for 0° ≤ θ ≤ 360°\n(A) 60° only  (B) 60° and 120°  (C) 120° and 240°  (D) 60° and 300°\n\n### Section B: Short Answer\n\n**Question 6**: A ladder 10 m long leans against a wall. If the foot of the ladder makes a 65° angle with the ground, how high up the wall does it reach?\n\n**Question 7**: In triangle XYZ, XY = 15 cm, YZ = 20 cm, and angle Y = 110°. Find XZ.\n\n**Question 8**: Prove: cos²θ - sin²θ = 2cos²θ - 1\n\n### Section C: WASSCE-Style\n\n**Question 9**: In triangle ABC, AB = 9 cm, BC = 12 cm, and angle ABC = 40°.\n(a) Find the area of triangle ABC.\n(b) Find AC.\n(c) Find the value of sin θ if θ is obtuse and sin θ = 3/5.\n\n---\n\n## Full Solutions\n\n**Q1**: C — tan 45° = 1\n\n**Q2**: B — In right triangle: O=5, H=13, so A=√(169-25)=√144=12. cos θ = A/H = 12/13.\n\n**Q3**: B — In Quadrant 2: sin positive, cos negative (CAST rule)\n\n**Q4**: B — c² = 25 + 49 - 2(5)(7)cos60° = 74 - 70(0.5) = 74 - 35 = 39; c = √39\n\n**Q5**: B — sin^(-1)(√3/2) = 60°. Sine positive in Q1 and Q2. Q1: 60°; Q2: 120°.\n\n**Q6**: Height = 10 × sin 65° = 10 × 0.9063 = 9.06 m\n\n**Q7**: XZ² = XY² + YZ² - 2(XY)(YZ)cos(Y)\n= 225 + 400 - 2(15)(20)cos(110°)\n= 625 - 600(-0.342)\n= 625 + 205.2 = 830.2\nXZ = √830.2 ≈ 28.8 cm\n\n**Q8**: RHS = 2cos²θ - 1\n= 2cos²θ - (sin²θ + cos²θ)\n= cos²θ - sin²θ\n= LHS ✓\n\n**Q9**:\n(a) Area = (1/2)(AB)(BC)sin(ABC) = (1/2)(9)(12)sin40° = 54 × 0.6428 = 34.7 cm²\n(b) AC² = 9² + 12² - 2(9)(12)cos40° = 81 + 144 - 216(0.766) = 225 - 165.5 = 59.5\nAC = √59.5 ≈ 7.71 cm\n(c) If sin θ = 3/5, then θ = sin^(-1)(3/5) = 36.87° or θ = 180° - 36.87° = 143.13°. Since θ is obtuse: θ ≈ 143.1°',
'practice', 5);
